+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
+    """
+    Cast outward control rays to carve room doors, then connect the rooms through the shortest doorway tree.
+
+    Input structure:
+    - Black background contains 4-5 disconnected blue hollow rectangles, each one cell thick.
+    - Every rectangle has exactly one red control pixel sitting just outside one wall cell, orthogonally adjacent to that wall.
+    - Each control points away from its own rectangle and its ray reaches another rectangle wall before the grid edge.
+
+    Rule:
+    - For each red control pixel, identify its adjacent blue wall and cast a ray outward, away from that rectangle, until the ray first hits another blue wall.
+    - Erase a 3-cell doorway on the hit wall, centered on the hit when possible and otherwise shifted inward just enough to fit; then erase the red control pixel.
+    - After all doorways are opened, treat each rectangle as a node with its opened doorway as its connection port. A direct edge exists only for rectangle pairs linked by a control-ray hit.
+    - Weight each direct edge by the Manhattan distance between the two doorway centers, choose the minimum spanning tree with the stated doorway-position tie breaks, and draw each chosen corridor in green using a horizontal-then-vertical orthogonal path that never paints intact blue walls.
+
+    Key insight:
+    - The red controls do not merely mark directions: they determine where doorways get carved on other rooms, and those dynamically created doorway positions then determine the graph used by the corridor MST.
